  "analysis": "Market reading: broad US equities are soft (A02 -1.2%, A10 -3.7%, A11 -2.2% over 20d) and the portfolio sits in a small -1.45% drawdown with no forced de-risking actions. The only equity groups with positive 20d returns are energy (A03 +10.7%) and health (A04 +5.9%); emerging (A12) is marginally positive. Gold has sold off sharply (-7.3% 5d) and carries the widest ETF forecast interval (9.7%) plus 30% annualized vol. Bonds (A09) have the narrowest interval of all 15 assets (3.4% 5d) — the model is most confident about them — making them the natural defensive anchor. All four single stocks show very wide intervals (9.2%-12.7%) and high idiosyncratic vol (26%-45%); the current portfolio already zeroes three of them, which I endorse. Since forecast medians carry no directional skill, I lean on interval width (reliable risk info), recent return data, and cross-asset substitution rather than directional bets. My changes: (1) rotate within us_broad from A10 (wider interval 5.98%, vol 12%, worst 20d) to A02 (narrowest equity interval 4.02%, vol 7.2%); (2) restore energy A03 from 1% toward its 5% baseline plus a small momentum tilt; (3) trim the most uncertain assets — gold A05 and tech stock A14 — toward/below baseline; (4) trim bonds A09 from 17.5% to 15%, still a large overweight vs the 11% baseline given their uniquely narrow interval; (5) hold cash at ~18%, above the 10% floor, as a buffer while equity direction is unclear. Net effect: equity exposure rises modestly from ~60.7% to ~64.6%, funded from bonds/gold, with total defensive (cash+bonds+gold) at ~35%. All group caps, ETF/stock caps, and the +15pp-above-baseline increase limit are respected; every >0.5pp deviation from baseline carries a rationale below.",
